 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#60922"
 msgid "Play HDR10+ videos as Dolby Vision"
 msgstr ""
 
-#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10plus"
+#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10plusmode"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#60923"
-msgid "For a TV that supports Dolby Vision but not HDR10+. An HDR10+ video is sent as Dolby Vision, with metadata made from its own HDR10+ data, scene by scene (the peak and the average brightness of each scene). The picture is the same HDR10 picture. What does not carry over from HDR10+: the tone curve and the target display that the studio chose, its saturation weight and its windows; in Dolby Vision Low-Latency (game mode), where Hades adapts each picture to the TV itself, the studio's tone curve is applied. Dolby Vision videos, HDR10 videos without HDR10+ and displays that take HDR10+ are not affected. Off by default. Takes effect from the next video."
+msgid "Sends HDR10+ videos as Dolby Vision to a TV that supports Dolby Vision, with metadata made from their own HDR10+ data, scene by scene (the peak and the average brightness of each scene). The picture is the same HDR10 picture. What does not carry over from HDR10+: the tone curve and the target display that the studio chose, its saturation weight and its windows; in Dolby Vision Low-Latency (game mode), where Hades adapts each picture to the TV itself, the studio's tone curve is applied. Off (the default): HDR10+ videos go out as HDR10+, or as HDR10 to a TV without HDR10+. Where the display has no HDR10+: only to a TV that supports Dolby Vision but not HDR10+, which would otherwise get plain HDR10. Always: also to a TV that supports both, if you prefer Dolby Vision. On such a TV the video's own HDR10+ is the more faithful picture, because the studio's tone curve, target display and windows do not carry over to Dolby Vision, so \"Where the display has no HDR10+\" is the recommended choice. Dolby Vision videos and HDR10 videos without HDR10+ are not affected. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
